=== entry b0b24cde22be
kind: translation
rules: transcripts
intent: Says past chats are T3 threads and names the tools that list, search, and read them, plus where this chat's ID comes from. Says search is not exhaustive and skips subagent threads, so step 3 lists rather than searches. Keep that one record is the user's own chat history.
--- upstream
Transcripts live at `~/.cursor/projects/<slug>/agent-transcripts/<uuid>/<uuid>.jsonl`, where `<slug>` is the workspace path with the leading slash dropped and each "/" turned into "-" (so `/Users/you/proj` becomes `Users-you-proj`). Every line is one chat message.
--- patched
Your chats are T3 threads. `t3_thread_list` lists this project's threads newest first (page with `cursor`, and pass `includeSubagents: true` for the child threads your subagents ran in), `t3_thread_search` searches their titles and content but is not exhaustive and skips subagent threads, and `t3_thread_read` reads one by its `threadId` (page with `afterPosition`, and use `view: "activity"` for tool calls). This chat's own `threadId` is the `currentThreadId` that `t3_thread_list` returns.

=== entry 92aaf8af3b00
kind: translation
rules: transcripts, delegate, model-unnamed, read-only
intent: The fan-out reads chats with the T3 thread tools. Upstream's corpus is every transcript file in the window, so the T3 corpus is every thread `t3_thread_list` returns in the window, paged with `cursor`, with subagent threads included when a parent in scope delegated the work (in T3 the child holds the tool calls its parent only summarizes; upstream's skip of subagent chats stays for children of out-of-scope parents). Each candidate is checked from its list fields and its activity; `t3_thread_search` is not exhaustive and skips subagent threads, so it only orders candidates and never drops one (upstream's "grep the topic first" was exhaustive over the files). Spawning is `delegate_task`; upstream's "fast, cheap model" becomes the `swarm workers` line (model-unnamed; the old `how explorer` role is retired). The shared-record investigators name no model, so they take `judgment and prose` per model-unnamed. "Agent mode because readonly strips MCP access" becomes never setting `runtimeMode`, with T3's reason. Step 5 reads a thread's activity view for what an agent did. Keep the steps, the per-chat schema, the scope rules, and the poteto-mode reasoning-level pointer.
--- upstream
1. Classify, then route. One specific prior chat to resume is the `session-pickup` playbook, not this. Turning habits into a durable skill is `automate-me`. A human-readable summary of your work is a different task. Recall loads working context across recent chats before you act. If the user already gave you a full state capsule (paths, branch, the change), use it and skip the mining.
2. Lock the scope before searching. Pin the window ("recent" is a real range, default the last 7 days), the topic if named, and the workspace (default the active one. Never read another project's transcripts without being asked). State the scope back. Never quietly turn "all" into "recent N".
3. Fan out across your chat history. Spawn parallel subagents on a fast, cheap model, each taking a slice of the corpus. Tell every subagent to order candidates by real modification time (`ls -t`) and never by UUID name, grep the topic first and then read only the matching chats and only their relevant regions, and skip the current chat plus obvious noise (subagent, eval, and test chats). Each returns the same schema, one block per chat: topic, the user's goal, decisions, open threads, struggles and corrections, and artifacts (PRs, tickets, branches), each citing the chat UUID. For one or two chats, skip the fan-out and search directly. The raw transcripts stay in the subagents. The main thread gets only their findings. Pick each subagent's reasoning level for the job, per the Subagents section of the **poteto-mode** skill.
4. Sweep the shared record whenever the topic names a feature, file, subsystem, area, or bug. This is the default, not a judgment call, and "my work on X" does not exempt it. Spawn one investigator subagent per source, in agent mode because readonly strips MCP access, and ask each "what's the current state, what's been tried and didn't hold, and what are users still reporting". Run them in parallel with the chat-history mining. Null results are findings. Skip an unavailable MCP and say so. Fold what comes back into the brief. Skip this step only for pure activity recall with no named target ("what did I do this week"), where your own history and live state are the entire answer.
5. Verify against live state. Take the PRs, branches, and tickets that the mining and the sweep surfaced and check them with `git` and `gh`. When the answer hinges on what an agent actually did (the tools it ran, files it read, errors it hit), read the full transcript, not just a trimmed local copy.
6. Write the brief to the contract below. Group by thread. Stay on the named topic.
--- patched
1. Classify, then route. One specific prior chat to resume is the `session-pickup` playbook, not this. Turning habits into a durable skill is `automate-me`. A human-readable summary of your work is a different task. Recall loads working context across recent chats before you act. If the user already gave you a full state capsule (paths, branch, the change), use it and skip the mining.
2. Lock the scope before searching. Pin the window ("recent" is a real range, default the last 7 days), the topic if named, and the workspace (default the active one. Never read another project's transcripts without being asked). State the scope back. Never quietly turn "all" into "recent N".
3. Fan out across your chat history. List the corpus first: page `t3_thread_list` with `cursor` until its threads fall outside the window, with `includeSubagents: true` when a chat in scope delegated work, since the child thread holds the tool calls its parent only summarizes. Every listed thread in the window is a candidate. `t3_thread_search` is not exhaustive and skips subagent threads, so use it only to put likely matches first, never to drop a candidate. Spawn parallel subagents with `delegate_task`, one call each in the same message, on the `swarm workers` line in {{snippet:models-file}}, else {{model:swarm workers}}, each taking a slice of the listed threads by `threadId`, with the line "Do not edit, create, commit, or push files." in its brief. Run `git status` once they return. Tell every subagent to order candidates by last activity (`updatedAt`) and never by ID, check every candidate for the topic (its title, branch, and PRs, then `t3_thread_read` with `view: "activity"` where those don't settle it) and then read only the matching chats and only their relevant regions, and skip the current chat (`currentThreadId`) plus obvious noise (eval and test chats, and subagent threads whose parent is out of scope). Each returns the same schema, one block per chat: topic, the user's goal, decisions, open threads, struggles and corrections, and artifacts (PRs, tickets, branches), each citing the chat UUID. For one or two chats, skip the fan-out and search directly. The raw transcripts stay in the subagents. The main thread gets only their findings. Pick each subagent's reasoning level for the job, per the Subagents section of the **poteto-mode** skill.
4. Sweep the shared record whenever the topic names a feature, file, subsystem, area, or bug. This is the default, not a judgment call, and "my work on X" does not exempt it. Spawn one investigator subagent per source with `delegate_task`, on the `judgment and prose` line in {{snippet:models-file}}, else {{model:judgment and prose}}. Never set `runtimeMode`: a narrower mode stalls the child on approvals no one can give, which cuts off its MCP access. Give each the line "Do not edit, create, commit, or push files.", and run `git status` once they return. Ask each "what's the current state, what's been tried and didn't hold, and what are users still reporting". Run them in parallel with the chat-history mining. Null results are findings. Skip an unavailable MCP and say so. Fold what comes back into the brief. Skip this step only for pure activity recall with no named target ("what did I do this week"), where your own history and live state are the entire answer.
5. Verify against live state. Take the PRs, branches, and tickets that the mining and the sweep surfaced and check them with `git` and `gh`. When the answer hinges on what an agent actually did (the tools it ran, files it read, errors it hit), read the full chat with `t3_thread_read` and `view: "activity"`, not just its messages.
6. Write the brief to the contract below. Group by thread. Stay on the named topic.
